# Doctrine Week 9. Converted from the old one-page lesson, section for section (I-X), in the author's order.

=== Introduction
--- The Doctrine of the Holy Spirit [title]
overline: Adult Sunday School · Faith Baptist Church of Plymouth County · Week 9
heading: The Doctrine of the Holy Spirit
sub: The Comforter: His nature, His promise, and His work in the life of every believer
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move through the study. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

--- The Comforter
scripture: John 16:7-12

=== I. His ministry, in plain terms
--- He reproves the world
lead: The Spirit of God, who was in the beginning, who inspired the prophets, who fell upon the men of God throughout the Old Testament, has other roles and responsibilities in the New Testament we are under.
p: He **reproves**, convicting the world of sin. That is, saved or unsaved people; that little voice is our conscience. He reproves the world of righteousness, silently pointing the lost toward faith in Christ. And of judgment, showing us that the prince of this world, that is, Satan, is wrong and will be destroyed.

--- The Spirit of truth
p: He is the Spirit of truth. When we hear the truth, especially from the Word of God, the thing that catches our attention and makes us listen is the Spirit. He moves saved believers into the path of the unsaved that they may hear the gospel: divine appointments. He softens hearts and opens ears. He helps lead people to salvation, if they respond.

--- Once we believe
p: Once we believe, He indwells us. He quickens our spirit so that we can know Him; this is regeneration. He sanctifies that new man inside us, sealing us unto redemption. The Bible says He is the earnest of our inheritance, the down payment of God upon us, His purchased possession.
p: He is the Spirit of adoption that lets us know we are the children of God, crying “Abba!” or “Father.” The Spirit testifies within us that we have eternal life.

--- He guides, remembers and intercedes
p: He guides us to all truth and lets us know truth from error. He brings to memory the word of God when we need it. He intercedes in our prayers, even when we don’t know what to pray for. He opens up knowledge to us and understanding when we read the Bible.
p: When we walk in the Spirit, the Bible says, we don’t fulfill the lusts of the flesh. He keeps us honest. We are His temple.
callout-honest: The Bible uses “Spirit” and “Ghost” interchangeably. The Holy Spirit and the Holy Ghost are one and the same.

=== II. The nature and attributes of the Spirit
--- The nature and attributes of the Spirit
lead: The Holy Spirit is a distinct person of the Godhead, possessing all the attributes of God.
- **Eternal**
- **Omnipotent**
- **Omniscient**
- **Omnipresent**

=== III. The promise of the Spirit
--- The promise of the Spirit
fig: Poured out upon all flesh | Joel 2:28 | A prophecy fulfilled in Acts 2.
fig: Another Comforter, forever | John 14:16-17 | Jesus promises the Holy Spirit as a permanent Comforter and Spirit of Truth.
fig: Power to be witnesses | Acts 1:8 | The Holy Spirit empowers believers to be witnesses of the gospel of Christ.

=== IV. Roles and purpose
--- Roles and purpose
fig: Guide into all truth | John 16:13 | The Spirit guides and reveals truth, not of Himself, but of Christ.
fig: Given to profit withal | 1 Corinthians 12:7 | The Spirit gives gifts to profit the body of Christ.
fig: Intercession in groanings | Romans 8:26 | The Spirit intercedes for believers in prayer.

=== V. The deity of the Spirit
--- The deity of the Spirit
lit: Thou hast not lied unto men, but unto God | Acts 5:3-4 | Lying to the Holy Spirit is equated with lying to God.
fig: Ye are the temple of God | 1 Corinthians 3:16 | The Holy Spirit is identified as God dwelling within believers.
fig: The eternal Spirit | Hebrews 9:14

=== VI. Salvation
--- Salvation
fig: The washing of regeneration | Titus 3:5 | The Holy Spirit is the agent of new birth.
fig: Born of the Spirit | John 3:5-6 | Being born of the Spirit is necessary for salvation.
fig: The mark of a believer | Romans 8:9 | The presence of the Spirit is the mark of a believer.

=== VII. Sanctification
--- Sanctification
fig: Chosen to salvation through sanctification | 2 Thessalonians 2:13 | The Spirit sets believers apart for God’s use.
fig: Elect through sanctification of the Spirit | 1 Peter 1:2
fig: The fruit of the Spirit | Galatians 5:22-23 | The Spirit produces character in believers.

=== VIII. Gifts of the Spirit
--- Gifts of the Spirit
group: Diversities of gifts, the same Spirit | The Spirit gives a variety of gifts for the edification of the body. | 1 Corinthians 12:4-11
group: Gifts differing according to grace | A list of gifts including prophecy, ministry, teaching, exhortation, giving, ruling, and mercy. | Romans 12:6-8
group: Gifted men to the church | Christ gives gifted men to the church. | Ephesians 4:11

--- Gifts and fruit
p: The gifts and the fruit of the Spirit are often discussed together, but they are different in purpose. **Gifts** are supernatural abilities given by God to a believer for the benefit of others and the church (teaching, helps, prophecy), given as the Spirit wills; not every believer has the same gifts.
p: **Fruit**, on the other hand, is the character produced by the Spirit living in a believer’s life. It is not an ability but a reflection of the Spirit’s work, and all believers are commanded to cultivate it. A person may have a powerful gift, but the true mark of spiritual maturity is the presence of the fruit.

=== IX. The fruit of the Spirit
--- The fruit of the Spirit
lead: Galatians 5:22-23: nine qualities, one fruit, produced in every believer who walks in the Spirit.
scripture: Galatians 5:22-23
tile: 1 | Love
tile: 2 | Joy
tile: 3 | Peace
tile: 4 | Longsuffering
tile: 5 | Gentleness
tile: 6 | Goodness
tile: 7 | Faith
tile: 8 | Meekness
tile: 9 | Temperance

=== X. Blasphemy of the Spirit
--- Blasphemy of the Spirit
lead: A specific, unforgivable sin, often understood as the hardened, final rejection of the Spirit’s testimony about Christ.
rev: Shall not be forgiven | Matthew 12:31-32 | A specific sin that is unforgivable, often interpreted as the hardened rejection of the Spirit’s testimony about Christ.
